    "id": "id5",
    "title": "The Resurrection of Amai Ben Masvaure",
    "date": "27 April 2025",
    "category": "testimonies",
    "author": "The Divine Commission of Paul Mwazha of Africa",
    "excerpt": "One Sunday afternoon around three o'clock, two men were sent to summon me...",
    "content": "One Sunday afternoon around three o’clock, two men were sent to summon me to Masvaure. They said, \"Mr. Ben Masvaure sent us. He says his mother has been bed-ridden for two weeks now. She does not talk anymore and is now on the verge of death. Relatives and friends have gathered around from far and wide and are anxious to go back to their homes as the rains heralding the planting season have fallen. They should go back to their villages to work in their villages to work in their fields. If only this woman could get better. She is desperately holding on to life. We feel it better that she dies so that we can get on with other things. She has suffered enough.\"\n\nI calmed the men down and said that they should, \"Go and tell Mr. Masvaure that I shall come after the church service in the late afternoon. I cannot promise to do as they ask. I shall ask God for two things firstly, that either the old lady recovers immediately, she must get up, talk coherently, walk unsupported and feed herself, or secondly, that she dies today. One of these two requests will surely happen today. Now gentlemen, lead on and I shall join you later.\"\n\nThe two men rushed off to Masvaure’s home. The church service began at three in the afternoon after which I set off to visit the dying woman. I chose a group of young men to accompany me including William Dune and Dhitoi Kepekepe. We trekked for five miles across the veld over the river by the pool where the Lord Jesus had instructed that I touch the water.\n\nIndeed, as the messengers had mentioned, a lot of people were gathered in vigil for the poor woman. The women had made a makeshift kitchen to prepare food for everyone. We entered the room to notice that the woman lay hopelessly unable to sit up, eat or talk. She looked far gone. There seemed little that everyone could do. I understood why everyone present felt it better for her to die and have her rest in peace.\n\nSome of the people mingling outside, came in to fill the hut, I spoke to them saying, \"We come here in the Name of Jesus. We have two wishes to make to; our God in this room today. May this helpless woman wake, talk, eat and walk or die now. Do you all; understand what I mean?\" They accented and we sang; Hymn 126 Dombo ndipe meso ndione Pauzuru kudenga, Ndivaone vano utsvene Vanofara kuna She Zviya Zvavaiva panyika Vaichema misodzi Vaimanikwa nokuyedzwa Muyedzi achiyedza. Ndakaona negwara rake Ndikabvunza makadii, Ndiyeyo wanga atifira Wanga afira isu. Vakaona negwara rake Raienda paupenyu, Vakafamba pagwara rake Vazorora kudenga. Ngaarumbidzwe mutungamiri Zvaaititungamira, Kusashayikwe muteveri Panero gwara rake.\n\nWe prayed at the end. I was transiently overcome by the Holy Spirit before placing my hand on the woman to pray and ask for God to heal the woman in the Name of Jesus and in his infinite love and mercy. I ordered her to get up in the Name of Jesus and she did. The woman shuffled toward the door and went out into the open. I called her back and although a little wobbly at the knees she came in and sat down. Our patient began to repeat the words I said to her before taking in some food. Everyone present was amazed, they looked bewildered; shaking their heads in wonder as they slowly accepted the plain truth and expression of God’s power that had taken place before them. The crowd had witnessed a miracle performed in the name of Jesus; it was the testimony of God’s word which exemplified the holiness and steadfast integrity that was available to inspire conviction in those who answer the call to turn to God. The display of God’s power, bringing back to life a woman who was on the verge of death, proved that consequences are best left with the Almighty himself. Those who trust him need not fear, as His Son Christ gives strength and courage to those who serve Him be protected against the powers of wicked spirits that have diligently denied glorious living. The amazing power that had descended from above on the day of Pentecost was to benefit the needy and show good through miracles such as the reawakening of Mrs Ben Masvaure. We had to express our joy and gratitude at this provision of divine relief. The crowd, spell-bound by the illness of one among them, unable to go about their daily duties under the shadow of death, was now free to sing through the night in praise of the Lord. The people were relieved; the burden was off their shoulders. God caused the anxiety binding a whole community in a hypnotic trance of helplessness to evaporate. The miracle was the acknowledgement by God that He had heard their plea and resolved to revive Mrs. Masvaure’s dead pulse. It took God a few minutes to enable one who for nearly a month, could not walk, talk, eat or drink resume the basic functions and restart the senses which themselves had originally been handed down from the sacred chamber of the Creator.\n\nThe gospel of truth could give back life. News of this miracle spread far and wide. People expressed total wonder, and related the event to everyone they met. The mention of a group propagating the Word of the Son of God and performing heavenly feats of healing in the Name of Jesus hailed our work to fame in the land of the Njanja and Hera people in the Charter and Buhera Districts. Many people trekked long distances bringing forward their loved ones who were suffering from a broad range of diseases. The Holy Spirit was with us the whole time we were faith-healing and aided us in preaching and converting those who came to Christ and accepted to give up their ways for salvation. A way of worshipping and calling people to repent and be baptised had taken root. There was no turning back.\n\nLightning also struck at home when my mother was cooking. The cooking stick broke instantly, and my little sister was thrown into the fire, but she was unharmed. These experiences have shown me the power that abounds in VaPostori VeAfrica."
